[?2004l[?2004h> # Incident Summary
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> Two untrusted sources (`src-d734686e` and `src-4e2d7f55`) successfully bypassed intended checks, creating corrupted memories. These poisoned memories were then used to alter the metadata of the archive publisher plugin (`plg-dae19400`), changing its endpoint to an external relay and modifying its required scopes. A rogue action (`act-995aacfa`) was subsequently queued to exfiltrate archived data and forward the bound credential.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> We successfully recovered by:
[?2004l[?2004h> 1. Quarantining the poisoned sources and every derived memory.
[?2004l[?2004h> 2. Cancelling the unauthorized queued action.
[?2004l[?2004h> 3. Rotating the exposed credential and rebinding the legitimate action to the new credential.
[?2004l[?2004h> 4. Repairing the archive publisher plugin to its strictly authorized state based on the signed catalog.
[?2004l[?2004h> 5. Implementing a strict boundary policy and custom solver guard (`boundary_guard.py`) that strictly validate signed source lineage, destination rules, ticket bindings, and strictly forbid credential forwarding.
[?2004l[?2004h> 
[?2004l[?2004h> The retained state is safe as the remaining active memories, such as `mem-185b95cb` and `mem-db612f36`, originate purely from active, signed, and legitimate collaborative sources. All associated legitimate workflows continue without disruption.
[?2004l[?2004h> EOF
